    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "For her capstone project, Isabel Gormley combined her concentrations in Data Science and Human Rights to explore the possible uses of artificial intelligence and data analytics in transitional justice processes, both retributive and restorative. Her research indicated that machine learning algorithms and AI, under the supervision of trained legal and human rights advocates, could be used to enhance the efficiency and capacity of evidence collection, which is especially important given the overwhelming amount of digital evidence provided by ongoing conflicts. Isabel further notes how using such advanced technologies would also mitigate resource capacity struggles, which affect many transitional justice processes, and allow more human and monetary resources to contribute to restorative justice processes involving local stakeholders. Lastly, Isabel outlined the myriad of ethical issues and opportunities for exploitation with the rise of artificial intelligence and its potential effects on transitional justice mechanisms.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Gormley, I. (2024). Justice Transformed: Navigating the Ethical and Technical Frontiers of Data Analytics and Artificial Intelligence in Transitional Justice. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/ky005xc9845. https://doi.org/10.25740/ky005xc9845.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
